<emu-clause id="sec-conformance">
  <h1>Conformance</h1>
  <p>
    This Standard defines two conformance classes: TEA server and TEA client.
    An implementation may conform to either or both.
  </p>

  <p>A conformant TEA server shall:</p>

  <emu-alg>
    1. Implement all operations defined in <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-api" title></emu-xref>, except, conditionally, those operations labelled as “normative optional” (see <emu-xref href="#step-authentication"></emu-xref>).
    2. Support the discovery of its API as defined in <emu-xref href="#sec-well-known-response" title></emu-xref>.
    3. [id="step-authentication"] Conform to <emu-xref href="#sec-authentication" title></emu-xref> if authentication is required for access to some or all of the implementation’s objects.
    4. Assign TEA UUIDs with the uniqueness and stability defined in <emu-xref href="#sec-uuid-scope" title></emu-xref>.
    5. Return payloads which conform to <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-data-model" title></emu-xref>.
  </emu-alg>

  <p>A conformant TEA client shall:</p>

  <emu-alg>
    1. Resolve Transparency Exchange Identifiers as defined in <emu-xref href="#sec-tei-resolution" title></emu-xref>.
    2. Authenticate, where a TEA server requires, as defined in <emu-xref href="#sec-authentication" title></emu-xref>.
    3. Send requests which conform to <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-api" title></emu-xref>.
  </emu-alg>

  <p>
    The verbal forms in this Standard follow ISO/IEC Directives, Part 2:
    <strong>shall</strong> and <strong>shall not</strong> indicate requirements;
    <strong>should</strong> and <strong>should not</strong> indicate recommendations;
    <strong>may</strong> and <strong>need not</strong> indicate permission; and
    <strong>can</strong> and <strong>cannot</strong> indicate possibility or capability.
    The verbal form <strong>must</strong> is used only for external constraints and
    does not express a requirement of this Standard.
  </p>
</emu-clause>
